WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
The first film followed a gold, GTP-loaded switch until cutting turned it slate.

2
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.200
If cutting ends that interval, what happens when something blocks the cut or speeds

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.840
it up? Use the same colors and the same switch.

4
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:19.823
Begin with the ordinary cycle.

5
00:00:21.243 --> 00:00:23.623
A teal loading event starts a gold interval.

6
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:30.303
Intrinsic cutting later removes the final phosphate, leaving GDP and ending the

7
00:00:30.303 --> 00:00:34.803
interval. This is our neutral reference for every perturbation.

8
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:40.387
Replace GTP with an analogue the protein can bind but cannot cut.

9
00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:43.267
Loading still turns the switch on.

10
00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:46.387
Nothing changes at the loading arrow.

11
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:52.567
The off event fails, so the gold interval persists after receptor input ends.

12
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.970
Cholera toxin reaches the same logic through Gs.

13
00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:04.470
Its modification obstructs Gs alpha's hydrolysis, so adenylyl cyclase remains active

14
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.650
and cyclic AMP stays elevated.

15
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.130
The timer stalls downstream of the receptor.

16
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:17.843
Now add extra RGS protein to an already-loaded G alpha that this RGS can regulate.

17
00:01:19.143 --> 00:01:20.683
All other parts begin matched.

18
00:01:21.923 --> 00:01:25.883
Should its active interval grow longer, grow shorter, or stay unchanged?

19
00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:37.267
Shorter. RGS accelerates cutting for the Gi and Gq families.

20
00:01:38.527 --> 00:01:42.787
The final phosphate leaves sooner, so the block returns to slate earlier.

21
00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:45.647
That family boundary matters.

22
00:01:46.927 --> 00:01:49.407
RGS does not have this effect on Gs.

23
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.200
Ras G12V breaks GAP-assisted cutting differently.

24
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.340
GAP may bind, but the catalytic arrangement fails.

25
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.320
This is catalytic failure, not missing GAP.

26
00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.180
Ras therefore remains GTP-bound far longer than normal.

27
00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:13.123
Ras Q61, shown beside it, also weakens cutting, even without GAP.

28
00:02:14.503 --> 00:02:17.343
Both mutants keep the gold interval abnormally long.

29
00:02:18.623 --> 00:02:21.903
They damage the off route, although their detailed defects differ.

30
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:26.957
Dominant-negative Ras S17N is the mirror image.

31
00:02:28.257 --> 00:02:31.857
It binds GTP poorly and fails to activate targets.

32
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:34.977
That failure sits on the loading side.

33
00:02:36.257 --> 00:02:39.497
It may also hold exchange factors away from normal Ras.

34
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.830
Sort every case by its changed arrow.

35
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:48.370
The analogue, toxin, and activating Ras mutants obstruct cutting.

36
00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.130
Extra RGS accelerates cutting only for its substrates.

37
00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.390
Dominant-negative Ras impairs loading.

38
00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.490
One cycle can therefore organize every case.

39
00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:06.813
This classification predicts direction, not the exact shape of every cell response.

40
00:03:08.233 --> 00:03:12.333
Receptor shutoff, target kinetics, and messenger clearance still contribute.

41
00:03:13.613 --> 00:03:15.233
The map keeps that limit visible.

42
00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:19.293
First locate the changed step, then judge the trace.

43
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:24.987
A lasting signal is therefore not mysterious once the cycle is mapped.

44
00:03:26.427 --> 00:03:30.027
Ask whether loading increased, cutting slowed, or clearance changed.

45
00:03:31.327 --> 00:03:34.307
The same map prevents blaming the receptor automatically.

46
00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:38.427
The position of the defect determines the prediction.
